          %h1#the_parallelogram_identity The Parallelogram Identity
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#inner_products"} Inner Products
              %li
                %a{:href => "#isometries_on_inner_product_spaces"} Isometries on inner product spaces
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %strong parallelogram identity
            is an identity which characterises those
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/norms"} norms
            which are the norms associated with
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inner+products"} inner products
          %p
            If length is known, angle can be defined by the
            = succeed ":" do
              %em cosine law
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi c
                    %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi a
                    %mn 2
                  %mo +
                  %msup
                    %mi b
                    %mn 2
                  %mo −
                  %mn 2
                  %mi a
                  %mi b
                  %mi cos
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  c^2 = a^2 + b^2 - 2 a b \cos C
          %p
            Although this formula could be used to define “angle” for any length (that is, norm), not every length admits a
            %em sensible
            notion of an angle. There are several ways to describe what the
            %em fundamental properties
            of angles should be. One is to say that angles should add:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi θ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi u
                  %mo ,
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo +
                  %mi θ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi v
                  %mo ,
                  %mi w
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi θ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi u
                  %mo ,
                  %mi w
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\theta(u,v) + \theta(v,w) = \theta(u,w)
          %p This needs careful interpretation since the angle between two vectors is slightly ambiguous: one can choose the internal angle or the external angle and it is not possible to make a consistent choice. However, modulo that uncertainty, the above is a reasonable property to insist on.
          %p
            A special case of this is when
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi w
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mi u
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} w = -u
          %div{:style => "text-align: center"}
            %svg{:height => "155", "se:nonce" => "59338", :width => "356", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/2000/svg", "xmlns:se" => "http://svg-edit.googlecode.com"}
              %g
